--TEST--
every `#[Command]` and `#[Option]` the program declares is readable back by naming `Core\Command` and `Core\Option`, so a usage text is generated rather than written — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, `rule:attributes/a-retrieval-matches-the-declaration-it-names` and `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The help below contains no text of its own: every name, every spelling
// and every sentence in it is read back out of the program's own
// `#[Core\Command]` and `#[Core\Option]` declarations. A retrieval names
// `Core\Command` or `Core\Option` and returns that attribute's payload, so
// **nobody writes usage text**.
//
// Two things are asserted, and the first is not a line below:
//
//   * **The table is the compilation.** `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`'s three compile errors — a
//     duplicate command name, two options sharing a spelling, an `#[Option]`
//     on a parameter whose declared type has no conversion from `string` —
//     are all reachable from this file, so a table that never grew a row, or
//     grew two rows for one name, does not reach the runtime at all.
//   * **Correctness is counted.** Four rows over three methods, every one of
//     them described, no two named alike — a retrieval that answered
//     plausibly for `deploy` and collapsed the two names one method declares
//     still fails the sweep.

// The command rule's own example, written as the rule writes it: a parameter is a
// positional argument unless it carries `#[Option]`, with no inference from
// defaults or types, and `uint $retries` is converted during matching.
class Deploy {
    #[Core\Command(name: "deploy", about: "Push the current build")]
    public static function deploy(
        tainted string $target,
        #[Core\Option(short: "n", long: "dry-run", about: "Print what would happen")] bool $dryRun,
        #[Core\Option] uint $retries = 3,
    ): uint {
        echo $target, $dryRun ? "!" : "", $retries, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// A second class, because the table is built out of `rule:programs/implementing`'s
// whole-program enumeration rather than out of one declaration site — and a
// second option carrying an `about` and no `short`, which is the other side of
// the bound the counters below name.
class Migrate {
    #[Core\Command(name: "migrate", about: "Apply pending migrations")]
    public static function migrate(
        #[Core\Option(about: "Say what would happen and stop")] bool $pretend,
    ): void {
    }
}

// `rule:attributes/repeatable`'s repetition, read as `nvs_types::commands` reads it: two
// `#[Command]`s on one method are two names for one implementation, which is
// what an alias is. Both become rows, so the roster below is four long over
// three methods.
class Seed {
    #[Core\Command(name: "seed", about: "Load the fixtures")]
    #[Core\Command(name: "fixtures", about: "Load the fixtures")]
    public static function seed(): void {
    }
}

// Every `Core\Option` field and `Core\Command`'s `about` is optional, so each read
// below uses `??` to give an absent one as the empty string.
class Help {
    // The short spelling is optional, so its absence is the empty string and
    // the rendered line simply has no `-x, ` in front of its long form.
    public static function short(string $short): string {
        if ($short == "") { return ""; }
        return "-" . $short . ", ";
    }

    public static function long(string $long): string {
        if ($long == "") { return ""; }
        return "--" . $long;
    }
}

// `rule:attributes/a-retrieval-matches-the-declaration-it-names`'s method spelling: a method reference names the method the
// `#[Command]` is attached to.
var $deploy = Core\Attributes::get<Core\Command>(Deploy::deploy);
var $migrate = Core\Attributes::get<Core\Command>(Migrate::migrate);

// `all<T>` rather than `get<T>`, because this method declares two rows and
// `get<T>` returns at most one — the alias is invisible to the singular form.
var $seeds = Core\Attributes::all<Core\Command>(Seed::seed);

array<string> $names = [
    $deploy?->name ?? "",
    $migrate?->name ?? "",
    $seeds[0]?->name ?? "",
    $seeds[1]?->name ?? "",
];

array<string> $abouts = [
    $deploy?->about ?? "",
    $migrate?->about ?? "",
    $seeds[0]?->about ?? "",
    $seeds[1]?->about ?? "",
];

int $described = 0;
foreach ($abouts as string $one) {
    if ($one != "") { $described = $described + 1; }
}

int $unique = 0;
foreach ($names as string $one) {
    int $same = 0;
    foreach ($names as string $other) {
        if ($one == $other) { $same = $same + 1; }
    }
    if ($same == 1) { $unique = $unique + 1; }
}

echo "commands=", Core\Arr::count($names), "\n";
echo "described=", $described, "\n";
echo "unique=", $unique, "\n";

// The same rule's parameter spelling: the same reference plus the parameter's own name,
// written as a literal. The three options are read as `Core\Option`, and the
// two that carry a sentence give it.
var $dryRun = Core\Attributes::get<Core\Option>(Deploy::deploy, "dryRun");
var $retries = Core\Attributes::get<Core\Option>(Deploy::deploy, "retries");
var $pretend = Core\Attributes::get<Core\Option>(Migrate::migrate, "pretend");

array<string> $optionAbouts = [
    $dryRun?->about ?? "",
    $retries?->about ?? "",
    $pretend?->about ?? "",
];

int $documented = 0;
foreach ($optionAbouts as string $one) {
    if ($one != "") { $documented = $documented + 1; }
}

int $shorts = 0;
if (Help::short($dryRun?->short ?? "") != "") { $shorts = $shorts + 1; }
if (Help::short($pretend?->short ?? "") != "") { $shorts = $shorts + 1; }

echo "options=", Core\Arr::count($optionAbouts), "\n";
echo "documented=", $documented, "\n";
echo "shorts=", $shorts, "\n";

// The match is by declaration, so a payload-less `#[Option]` and a parameter
// carrying no attribute at all read differently. `$target` is positional and
// has no `Core\Option`, so it is `none`. `$retries` is an option with no
// fields, and its empty payload is `some`.
var $target = Core\Attributes::get<Core\Option>(Deploy::deploy, "target");
echo "positional=", $target != null ? "some" : "none", "\n";
echo "bare option=", $retries != null ? "some" : "none", "\n";

// The help itself. Every word below came out of a declaration; the layout is
// all this file contributes, and the generated help of
// `rule:tooling/command-run-dispatches-and-help-is-generated` replaces even that.
echo "\n";
echo $names[0], " - ", $abouts[0], "\n";
echo "  ", Help::short($dryRun?->short ?? ""), Help::long($dryRun?->long ?? ""), "  ", $optionAbouts[0], "\n";
echo $names[1], " - ", $abouts[1], "\n";
echo "  ", Help::short($pretend?->short ?? ""), $optionAbouts[2], "\n";
echo $names[2], " - ", $abouts[2], "\n";
echo $names[3], " - ", $abouts[3], "\n";
--EXPECT--
commands=4
described=4
unique=4
options=3
documented=2
shorts=1
positional=none
bare option=some

deploy - Push the current build
  -n, --dry-run  Print what would happen
migrate - Apply pending migrations
  Say what would happen and stop
seed - Load the fixtures
fixtures - Load the fixtures
